    ":question/id": "918c847a-dee1-5e90-aa75-e08ca9d39fd8",
    ":question/math-academy-id": "q-66811",
    ":question/problem": "Given that ${3}^{4-2x}=\\sqrt{2},$ find the value of $x$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Taking ${log}_{3}$ of both sides of the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{4-2x} & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {log}_{3}\u2061({3}^{4-2x}) & ={log}_{3}\u2061(\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ 4-2x & ={log}_{3}\u2061({2}^{1/2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n4-2x=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}\u2061(2)\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula and then solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-2x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(2)}{2\\operatorname{log}\u2061(3)} \\\\ -2x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(2)}{2\\operatorname{log}\u2061(3)}-4 \\\\ x & =2-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(2)}{4\\operatorname{log}\u2061(3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above on a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx\u22481.842\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places"
  },
