    ":question/id": "d436286b-8ea5-5b92-b174-4ebb9b14bf53",
    ":question/math-academy-id": "q-973",
    ":question/problem": "Given that ${11}^{4-y}=\\sqrt[4]{3},$ find the value of $y$  rounded to $3$ decimal places.",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Taking ${log}_{11}$ of both sides of the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{11}^{4-y} & =\\sqrt[4]{3} \\\\ {log}_{11}\u2061({11}^{4-y}) & ={log}_{11}\u2061(\\sqrt[4]{3}) \\\\ 4-y & ={log}_{11}\u2061({3}^{1/4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n4-y=\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{11}\u2061(3)\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula and then solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-y & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(3)}{4\\operatorname{log}\u2061(11)} \\\\ -y & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(3)}{4\\operatorname{log}\u2061(11)}-4 \\\\ y & =4-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(3)}{4\\operatorname{log}\u2061(11)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above on a calculator gives\n\n$$\ny\u22483.885\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."
  },
